@@steps limit=8 mid=3

## Controls
@@control mode
What is made of the noise: a `height` map in gray, an `overworld` of water, grass and mountain, a `zoom` into the overworld, or the zoom with two noises mixed, `double` [[c10:23-24 c11:31-44 c12:77-90 c13:41-44]].
@@control octaves
In the talk’s words, how many different gradients it is mixing in [[t:154]].
@@control gain
In the talk’s words, how long the various gradients last [[t:155]]. In our noise, each further layer’s strength is the one before it times this [[own]].
@@control lacunarity
In the talk’s words, it adds in randomness [[t:156-158]]. In our noise, each further layer’s frequency is the one before it times this [[own]].
@@control frequency
In the talk’s words, how frequently each of the octaves peaks [[t:159]].

## What it produces, measured
@@measured

The overworld’s land is not one piece: {{c11.regions.median}} regions at the median, with water between them [[run:c11.regions.median]]. A map at the median is {{c11.water.median}} water, {{c11.grass.median}} grass and {{c11.mountain.median}} mountain [[run:c11.water.median c11.grass.median c11.mountain.median]]. The zoom’s last frame is a small window of the noise, and what it holds varies widely: its floor share runs from {{c12.floor_share.min}} to {{c12.floor_share.max}} over the seeds [[run:c12.floor_share.min c12.floor_share.max]]. The height map is all floor, so its numbers say only how big the map is [[own]].

## Where it fails
@@fails

Our criteria are written for maps to be walked across. An overworld isn’t one until something decides where a traveler can go [[own]].

## What it combines with
@@combines

In the questions after the talk, its author suggests mixing several noise maps together, or running an erosion simulation over one [[t:313-314 t:322-324]].

